    bodyMarkdown: "## Definitions\n\n| **Term** | **Definition**                                                                                       |\n|----------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Document | Useful information such as a procedure or a form.                                                    |\n| Record   | Evidence of the results of following a procedure (e.g., a completed inventory form or batch record). |\n| GDP      | Good Documentation Practices.                                                                        |\n| ALCOA    | The principle that records be Attributable, Legible, Contemporaneous, Original, and Accurate.        |\n\n## Associated Documents / References\n\n- Records and audit trail in CannaQMS\n\n- Document Control SOP\n\n- 21 CFR Part 11 (electronic records and signatures)\n\n## Responsibilities\n\n| **Role**                     | **Responsibility**                              |\n|------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------|\n| Quality / Management         | Trains personnel on GDP and enforces its use.   |\n| Supervisor / Department Lead | Implements and enforces GDP in their work area. |\n| All Employees                | Follow the requirements in this procedure.      |\n\n## Procedure\n\n1.  **Document all activities**\n\n- Documentation of all activities is required as evidence the product was made to internal and external requirements. If it isn't documented, it didn't happen.\n\n2.  **ALCOA principles**\n\n- Records must be Attributable, Legible, Contemporaneous (recorded when the activity occurs), Original, and Accurate, with sufficient detail.\n\n3.  **Electronic records in CannaQMS**\n\n- Entries in CannaQMS are automatically time-stamped and attributed to the signed-in user, and significant actions are signed with a 21 CFR Part 11 e-signature (identity, meaning, timestamp). The system retains an audit trail.\n\n- An unsigned or incomplete record is not evidence of a completed task.\n\n4.  **Paper records (where used)**\n\n- Complete paper records in indelible black or blue ballpoint pen; do not use pencil or erasable ink. Entries are made at the time the task is performed and are signed/initialed and dated.\n\n5.  **Corrections**\n\n- Write-overs are not permitted. To correct an error, draw a single line through it so the original remains legible, then initial and date the correction.\n\n6.  **References and traceability**\n\n- When documents are used together (e.g., an SOP and its form), each references the other using formal document or record numbers so entries are traceable.\n\n7.  **Review**\n\n- Records are reviewed by a knowledgeable person (e.g., the department lead/supervisor); the review signature/e-signature confirms the review took place.\n\n## Change History\n\n| **Revision** | **Description of Change** | **Release Date** |\n|--------------|---------------------------|------------------|\n| A            | New document              | [ ]            |",
